// Original work, written for STORYMACHINE_STRUCTURAL_FORM_EXPERIMENT_2026-08-03.
// Kishotenketsu arm, Pair 2 (n=16 scenes — at/above doctor.ts's
// ARC_DED_MIN_SCENES=15, so this pair, unlike Pair 1, exercises the
// continuous arcIncoherenceDeduction). Follows structure-presets.ts's
// kishotenketsu template: Ki 0-25% (scenes 1-4, no antagonism), Sho 25-50%
// (scenes 5-8, discovery not confrontation), Ten 50-75% (scenes 9-12, twist
// from outside the conflict axis — here, a historical-society letter, a third
// perspective), Ketsu 75-100% (scenes 13-16, synthesis, no winners/losers).
// Scenes 1-3 are textually identical to the matched control
// (pair2-three-act-control.fountain) — both templates want pure
// establishment before any conflict signal; divergence begins at scene 4,
// exactly where the beat maps split (Ki still running vs. Inciting Incident).

INT. AMARO CONSERVATION STUDIO - MORNING

Tall north-facing windows, even gray light. YUKI AMARO, mid-30s, leans over a slanted table with a bone folder and a jeweler's loupe, easing a brittle fold flat on a hundred-year-old atlas page. Wheat paste, brushes, weighted sandbags arranged with surgical precision.

YUKI
(not looking up)
Come around from the left. The light's better and you won't cast a shadow on the seam.

She works the fold another millimeter, exhales.

INT. STUDIO - STORAGE ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Shelves of flat archival boxes, each labeled in careful block print. Yuki slides one free: THORN COLLECTION — 1947 REPRINT, VOL. 3. She holds it a moment before carrying it out, the way you'd hold something that used to belong to someone.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - LATER

DEV OKAFOR, 20s, apprentice, sets two coffees down without being asked and peers at the open atlas.

DEV
Thorn's the one you trained under?

YUKI
Nineteen years. He restored this exact volume in '91, before I ever touched a bone folder. I'm just re-lining page thirty-four. Foxing's spread since the last treatment.

DEV
Feels like restoring someone's restoration.

YUKI
It usually is, eventually. That's the job.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - AFTERNOON

Yuki tilts page 34 under raking light. Along one printed border line, the paper is cockled — very slightly, very evenly — in a way ordinary handling wouldn't cause. She notes it in her treatment log without alarm, in the same careful hand as everything else.

YUKI
(to herself, writing)
Cockling along northeast border, page thirty-four. Consistent with... something. Not sure yet.

She underlines "not sure yet" twice and moves on.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - DAY

Yuki flips through one of Thorn's old restoration ledgers, decades of his handwriting narrowing and slanting more with age. Beside the entry for page 34, dated 1991, a single line: "Leave as found. Do not correct."

YUKI
(reading it twice)
That's not how you taught me to treat foxing.

DEV
Maybe it wasn't foxing.

INT. STUDIO - ARCHIVE SHELF - DAY

Dev hands ledgers down off a high shelf while Yuki lays five of them open across the table, each from a different decade — 1968, 1979, 1991, 2003, 2011. Beside a different border page in each one, the same instruction, in Thorn's hand, aging but unmistakably consistent: "Leave as found."

DEV
Five different pages. Five different years. Same handwriting, same four words.

YUKI
He never repeated himself in forty years of notes. Except this.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - EVENING

The five flagged pages laid side by side under matching lamps. Yuki and Dev trace each anomaly with a fingertip — always a border, always redrawn a few careful kilometers off true, always somewhere near a mountain pass.

DEV
It's the same kind of error every time. That's not an error.

YUKI
No. It's a pattern with very good manners.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - NIGHT

Yuki alone, the studio dark but for one lamp. She holds page 34 close to the bulb, angling it, and for the first time sees the faint ghost of a second, doubled line running just beneath the printed border.

YUKI
(quiet)
There you are.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - NIGHT (CONT'D)

Yuki runs a UV loupe slowly along the seam. Beneath the printed ink, a second inked line fluoresces pale blue — and beside it, coordinates in Thorn's own microscript, small enough to hide in the border's shadow. She begins transcribing them into her log, hand steady, unhurried.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - LATER THAT NIGHT

Dev cross-references the transcribed coordinates against a modern gazetteer on a laptop, scrolling, then stopping.

DEV
This traces a footpath through the pass. It's never been on any published version of this map. Not the '47 original, not any reprint.

YUKI
A route nobody printed on purpose.

INT. STUDIO - STORAGE ROOM - NIGHT

Tucked flat at the bottom of the THORN COLLECTION box, beneath tissue paper, Yuki finds a folded, brittle letter — a historical society's 1974 note of thanks to "the cartographers who quietly kept certain mountain passes off the official record during the occupation years, so that families could cross unseen." No signature, but the same hand has annotated one margin: "Passed to E.T., in trust."

YUKI
(reading, very still)
He wasn't hiding a mistake. He was keeping a promise.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - NIGHT (CONT'D)

The atlas open on the table between them. Yuki sets the letter down beside it, gently, like a second exhibit rather than evidence.

YUKI
Every page I've been "correcting" toward accuracy for a year — he redrew those borders on purpose, decades ago, to protect people who needed a way through that no official map would show.

DEV
So the flaw was the point.

YUKI
The flaw was the whole point.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - DAY

Yuki lays out two acetate overlays on a light table — one tracing the true 1947 survey line, one tracing Thorn's altered line — and begins drafting a facsimile that will carry both, with a short accompanying note explaining each.

YUKI
We don't erase his line. We just stop pretending it was an accident.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - DAY

Yuki and Dev work side by side at the light table, trimming and aligning the two overlays, passing tools back and forth without needing to ask. The atlas, mid-restoration, looks more honest than it has in years.

DEV
Feels less like fixing it now. More like finishing it.

YUKI
That's the job too, apparently.

INT. REGIONAL ARCHIVE - READING ROOM - AFTERNOON

Yuki presents the finished facsimile and a short written account to an ARCHIVIST at a long reading table. The archivist turns the pages slowly, reaches the doubled border, and nods once — recognition, not scandal.

ARCHIVIST
We'll catalogue this as a dual-layer conservation. Both lines documented, both dated, both his.

YUKI
That's exactly right.

INT. STUDIO - MAIN ROOM - DUSK

Yuki closes the now-empty THORN COLLECTION box and sets it back on the shelf. Beside it, she places a new box, freshly labeled: AMARO & THORN — ONGOING. She rests her hand flat on the lid a moment before switching off the lamp.
